A||||14||RZURCTN||Clutch hang-up?||||1||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||09:42 AM||||I am having a little problem with the clutch in my 4 speed hanging up on engagement.  It disengages fine, but when released it only travels halfway back.  I can then reach down and grab the "arm" for the clutch fork and pull it the rest of the way back.  Could the clutch just need to be bled?  I did manage to run the slave all the way out (travel) when it was off the tranny, while pushing the clutch to start the car.  I never disconnected the slave lines or anything like that.  I also noticed that the rod doesn't go straight into the "arm", it sets at an angle.  Could this be a problem too?  Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||12-09-1999||10:52 AM||||If you are talkihg about the arm on the tranny it's not supposed to return all the way back that is what this self adjusting bit is all about. I gather that your clutch works fine. If this is  the case everything is working just as it is supposed to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||12-09-1999||10:56 AM||||Sounds like your clutch master cylinder is shot.  I had this same problem with my 88 Coupe and that's what it turned out to be.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||12:00 PM||||When released, the clutch is not fully engaging.  I can block the wheel, put it in gear, get out, walk to the back, and pull on the fork arm.  As I do this, the clutch begins to engage more and it wants to roll.  The problem is that the slave is not returning far enough to allow the clutch to engage.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||12-09-1999||12:39 PM||||Could be the throwout bearing is binding on the input shaft.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||12-09-1999||01:20 PM||||<BR>The slave piston will only return as far as it's being pushed (by the clutch assembly). In other words, the slave piston is not going to return on it's own and <B>pull</B> the clutch fork with it. I would guess this problem has nothing to do with the hydraulics or the bleeding of the system. The suggestion of a possibly binding throwout bearing is good. Also, any chance that the clutch fork is bent and therefore causing binding somewhere?||||reg||4
Z||000006||dogtired||12-09-1999||01:48 PM||||The last sentance in your original post? If you are speaking of the rod that goes from your clutch pedal to the master cyl? That particular rod could be upsidedown. The end that goes on the pedal side is offset to the rod and would cause an angle if mounted wrong. (just pull the pin and turn it over, put the pin back in) I don't think this would cause or solve your problem, but could be the angle you speak of? Good Luck.||||reg||10
Z||000007||Phil||12-09-1999||03:08 PM||||I think there is a bit of confusion about exactly what your asking( at least for me). Are you talking about the foot pedal not returning or the arm on the tranny?||||reg||1
Z||000008||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||11:18 PM||||I am speaking of the arm on the tranny(sorry for the confusion).  It's not returning far enough to allow the clutch to engage.  I know I lubed the shaft that the throw out bearing rides on, and made sure that it traveled freely.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||12-10-1999||06:36 AM||||Does it look like the clutch fork is bent?||||reg||1
Z||000010||RZURCTN||12-10-1999||08:36 AM||||No.  After i pull back on the on the fork lever I can't push it forward by hand.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||12-10-1999||09:32 AM||||Four words,<BR>Really big rubber band.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||12
Z||000012||Phil||12-10-1999||09:46 AM||||Just wondering- Does the arm go back if you retract the slave cylinder push rod being careful not to touch the clutch arm (thinking of slave binding)? It's always better to eliminate all external trouble spots before going inside.||||reg||1
Z||000013||RZURCTN||12-10-1999||04:28 PM||||I'm not sure on that.  I had to get the Fiero out of the shop for the weekend.  I tried bleeding the slave and that didn't help.  Looks like I'll have to get inside of it.  Oh well, I'll probably be pulling the engine back out soon anyway.  It's having too much trouble for an engine that is supposed to run fine.   Thanks a lot guys!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Sage||12-10-1999||04:39 PM||||Had this problem with 88Coupe till the slave was"back bled"  i.e. push slave rod(manually)back into cylinder and keep preasure on it while crack open the bleed nipple.  Close the nipple while keeping the rod in.  After doing this, it was fine.  Previously it would not let the clutch fully engage and you got slippage, especially noticable in 5th gear.  Now it does not slip period.||||reg||1
